WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.070 align:start position:0%
 
we spent  three years in ivedan national park in  northeastern argentina  the cappuccino cedars are distributed  throughout south america  and they don't appear to have any clear  geographic barriers that prevent them  from exchanging genes  these various cappuccino species are  genetically very similar  they breed in the same habitat and they  eat the same food  and yet the males of these various  species differ in plumage coloration and  song  terbek and her team focused on two types  of cappuccinos  the tawny bellied seed eater and the  ibera sea eater which was undiscovered  until 2001. these two species  along with eight others split from a  single common ancestor  within the last million years this  relatively  recent rapid expansion occurred even as  they shared practically the same  neighborhood  normally for speciation to occur  organisms have to be confined to  different geographic regions  in order to accumulate genetic  differences and we've seen this process  occur in other radiations such as  darwin's finches where  from a single common ancestor they've  formed many different species that are  confined to different islands in the  galapagos  capuchino sea eaters in general and the  iwasivita and tanibelli cedar are  confined to continental south america  and they lack any obvious spatial or  temporal barriers to reproduction  it's unclear what kind of barriers  maintain these two species  in the same geographic area and keep  them distinct from one another  we searched for nest of the ebitda  seeder and the tawny bellied seed eater  we marked individual birds with unique  combination of color bands  and we collected blood samples from  adults and nestlings these two species  we presented territorial males of the  ebedasider and the tawny bellied seeder  with painted mounts and song recordings  that mimicked their own species song and  plumage  as well as that of the other cappuccino  species and recorded their aggressive  responses  both cappuccino species responded most  aggressively to  the song and plumage of their own  species and less aggressively  when the song or plumage belonged to the  other species which suggests that both  of these traits  are jointly used in order to distinguish  one another in territorial interactions  and avoid interbreeding we  sequence the dna of the two species in  order to determine how they differ  on a genetic level as well as analyze  patterns of meat choice in order to  assess whether these two cappuccinos  ever interbreed in the wild  and to our surprise we found that even  though these species breed side by side  and they share the same resources in  yubata national park  the iburasi duder and the tawny belly  cedar are only ever  mated with members of their own species  since the two cappuccino species are so  genetically similar  terbeck's team essentially was searching  for needles in a haystack  the iberassi duder and the tawny belly  cedar are almost  identical on a genetic level and they  can only be distinguished by three tiny  regions of their genome that only  contain 12 genes  three of these genes are known to be  involved in plumage coloration in other  bird species  and have likely contributed to the  diverse plumage patterns that we observe  in this group  ebitda seeded are likely formed as a  result of past hybridization between  cappuccino lineages  and the subsequent reshuffling of  genetic variation  into novel combinations that encode  differences in plumage patterning and  potentially male song differences in  song and plumage alone may be sufficient  to maintain species boundaries  extremely early in divergence  [Music]  work remains to determine if  cappuccino's songs diverge  genetically or if their differences are  entirely learned  it's also unknown whether the distinct  cappuccino genes associated with plumage  are actively expressed for now the  bera and tawny bellied seed eaters will  likely continue mating within their own  species  despite sharing the same turf and almost  all the same genes  as the saying goes birds of a feather  flock together  [Music]

